 "intro": "Near Dyrrachium (Durrës), on the Adriatic coast of Greece, 48 BC. You are Julius Caesar, trying to besiege the larger army of Pompey with siege lines seventeen miles long.",
 "debrief": "In Caesar’s account, both armies suffered: Caesar’s men ate bread made of roots, Pompey’s horses starved. Two Gallic deserters told Pompey where the lines were unfinished at the southern end by the sea, and he attacked there by land and sea, routing the Ninth Legion. Caesar’s counter-attack went wrong and his men panicked; he lost about 1,000 men and 32 officers. Pompey did not follow up, and Caesar remarked that the enemy would have won if they had a commander who knew how to win. He marched away to Thessaly, and beat Pompey at Pharsalus a month later. The other choices are what-ifs.",
